# Check Helm Environment

Display Helm environment variables:

```bash
helm env
```

---

# Check Current Repositories

Initially:

```bash
helm repo list
```

Output:

```bash
Error: no repositories to show
```

---

# Add a Repository (Example: Bitnami)

```bash
helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
```

Update repositories:

```bash
helm repo update
```

Verify:

```bash
helm repo list
```

Output:

```bash
NAME      URL
bitnami   https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
```

---

# Search Available Charts

Search all charts:

```bash
helm search repo
```

Search for Nginx:

```bash
helm search repo nginx
```

Example:

```bash
helm search repo nginx
```

Output:

```text
NAME                    CHART VERSION   APP VERSION   DESCRIPTION
bitnami/nginx           21.x.x          1.29.x        NGINX Open Source web server
```

---

# Install a Chart

```bash
helm install my-nginx bitnami/nginx
```

Check the release:

```bash
helm list
```

---

# Uninstall a Release

```bash
helm uninstall my-nginx
```

---

# Check Helm Help

```bash
helm --help
```

---

## Verify Kubernetes Connection

Before using Helm with a cluster, make sure `kubectl` is configured correctly:

```bash
kubectl cluster-info
kubectl get nodes
```

If these commands work, Helm will be able to interact with your Kubernetes cluster.

Since you're learning Helm deeply, after installation the next logical topics are:

1. Helm architecture
2. Helm charts and directory structure
3. `Chart.yaml`, `values.yaml`, and `templates/`
4. Built-in template objects
5. Go templating in Helm
6. Installing, upgrading, rolling back, and uninstalling releases
7. Creating and publishing your own Helm charts with real-world DevOps examples.
